-- ============================================================================
-- PD1 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Nodal Analysis & Well Performance, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE NODE. Where the two curves meet, how many times,
-- and whether the meeting holds. It is the only one of the three tiers whose
-- question can have more than one answer, which is why the split was made this
-- way.
--
-- A NODE CAN HAVE TWO SOLUTIONS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM HOLDS. When the column
-- outweighs the reservoir at low rate the residual STARTS POSITIVE, crosses
-- zero on the way down and crosses back on the way up. The lower crossing is
-- the heading branch and a well sitting there is driven away from it by any
-- disturbance. A learner who reports the first intersection a solver returns
-- gets the unstable one. On the capstone the two crossings are 905.3368 and
-- 929.9821 stb/d, 24.6453 stb/d apart on a well whose open flow is 3848.0055.
--
-- STABILITY IS A STATEMENT ABOUT A DIFFERENCE OF SLOPES, and neither term is
-- required to have any particular sign. The shortcut that a well is stable
-- right of the tubing minimum and unstable left of it is nearly right and
-- fails exactly where the answer is hard. The teaching well ESCRAVOS-9 is the
-- counterexample and it flows: its stable crossing sits 792.375394 stb/d to
-- the LEFT of its minimum, with an outflow slope of -0.28613256 psi per stb/d
-- against an inflow slope of -0.45150309, giving a residual slope of
-- +0.16537053. The crossing holds not because the outflow rises but because it
-- falls more slowly than the inflow does.
--
-- THE RECORDED ENGINE DEFECT THIS TIER OWNS, AND IT IS THE MOST USEFUL
-- SENTENCE IN THE COURSE. `solveNodeCore` scans a grid, and a grid has a
-- resolution. At its documented default `nGrid` of 40 the capstone's samples
-- sit 98.4695 stb/d apart, both crossings fall inside one interval, and the
-- engine reports a LIVE well DEAD with every residual it computed correct. The
-- failure is not even monotone in grid density: 40 finds none, 60 finds both,
-- 100 finds NONE AGAIN, and 200, 400, 900 and 4000 all find both and agree on
-- the same operating rate to the last digit. THE SCAN FAILS LONG BEFORE THE
-- STABILITY SLOPE TEST DOES, because the slope test never gets a bracket to
-- run on. The capstone is therefore graded at 900 grid points and says so.
--
-- WHY A SWEEP BEATS A POINT. Near tangency the payoff is violently asymmetric.
-- Sixty psi off the capstone's wellhead pressure, at 1176 psia, opens the
-- stable window from 24.6453 to 504.1220 stb/d. Twelve psi the other way, at
-- 1248 psia, kills the well outright. That asymmetry is not a quirk of one
-- well, it is what two nearly tangent curves always give, and it is the reason
-- an answer to a real design question is a curve rather than a number.
-- ============================================================================

